\title{ccwbalance: artificial-censoring diagnostics for clone-censor-weight
analyses}
\description{
Diagnostics for the artificial-censoring weights of clone-censor-weight
(CCW) analyses: interval and cumulative risk-set reconstruction balance
(RRB) with distributional (KS) and multivariate (Mahalanobis) versions,
Jackson-type censoring balance, a conditional parametric null reference
for the maximum absolute imbalance (MaxIB), an individual-level
bootstrap, between-strategy (including end-of-grace) standardised mean
differences, weight-support summaries, plots, tools to build the input
data and a simulation data-generating mechanism.
}
\details{
\subsection{Notation}{

Follow-up is divided into intervals \code{I_k}, \verb{k = 0, 1, ..., K - 1}
(column \code{time}). \code{D_k} indicates artificial censoring at the end of
interval \code{k} (column \code{censored_next}), \code{R_k} membership of the risk set
at the start of interval \code{k}, and \code{H_k} the history up to \code{L_k} and
\verb{A_\{k-1\}}. \verb{pi_k = P(D_k = 0 | R_k = 1, H_k)} is the probability of
remaining uncensored (column \code{pi_hat}) and \code{q_k} the stabilising
numerator, common to all individuals in a stratum (column \code{q_hat}). A
stratum is a strategy x interval combination. The cumulative weight is
\verb{W_k = prod_\{l <= k\} q_l / pi_l = Q_k W^u_k}, with \verb{Q_k = prod_\{l <= k\} q_l} and the unstabilised weight \verb{W^u_k = prod_\{l <= k\} 1 / pi_l}.
}

\subsection{Data contract}{

The diagnostic functions take long data with one row per individual x
strategy x interval in which the individual is in the risk set of the
strategy (\code{R_k = 1}). Column names can be changed through the function
arguments. Required columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{id}: individual identifier;
\item \code{strategy}: strategy label (factor or character);
\item \code{time}: interval index \code{k} (\verb{0, 1, ...});
\item \code{censored_next}: \code{D_k} (1 if artificially censored at the end of
interval \code{k}, else 0);
\item \code{censor_possible}: whether artificial censoring can occur in the row;
\item \code{phase}: kind of artificial censoring possible in the row (e.g.
\code{"no_init"}, \code{"grace_end"}, \code{"sustain"}, \code{"none"}); together with
\code{strategy} it defines the censoring-model groups;
\item \code{event_next}: outcome in interval \code{k} (\code{NA} when censored);
\item history features \code{f(H_k)}, e.g. \code{X}, \code{L}, \code{L_lag}, and those added by
\code{\link[=make_history_features]{make_history_features()}}.
}

Optional columns: \code{ltfu_next} (loss to follow-up in interval \code{k}) and
\code{A} (treatment \code{A_k}, used to orient the coupling of \code{\link[=rrb_null]{rrb_null()}}).
\code{\link[=fit_censoring_model]{fit_censoring_model()}} adds the weight columns \code{pi_hat}, \code{q_hat},
\code{w_prev_unstab}, \code{w_prev_stab}, \code{w_ref} and \code{w_post}. \code{\link[=ccw_clone]{ccw_clone()}}
builds data in this format from one row per individual or from
person-period data, and \code{\link[=simulate_ccw]{simulate_ccw()}} simulates it.
}
